20:57:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I appreciate you framing it that way, because there is actually a kernel of truth in the idea that environment shapes development. That's not controversial—nutrition, education, healthcare, safety, and stimulation all influence how a person develops cognitively. The problem is that you're taking a very broad, correct idea and forcing it through a narrow, incorrect lens: that *latitude* or *temperature* is the environmental factor that matters. When you say "where you grow up shapes how sharp you turn out," I fully agree. But "where" doesn't just mean how hot it is. It means whether you had enough iodine as a child, whether you had access to books, whether your schools were resourced, whether you grew up in a zone of political stability or constant crisis, whether your parents had the economic security to spend time reading to you. These are the environmental factors that actually have measurable impacts on cognitive development, and they track much more with poverty and inequality than with degrees of latitude. And here's the thing: these factors are not evenly distributed around the world by accident. The reason many equatorial countries face higher poverty and weaker educational infrastructure is not because of the heat—it's because of historical exploitation, colonial extraction, and ongoing global economic structures that disadvantage them. That's not a conspiracy; it's documented history. The "common sense" you're describing has its roots in exactly that history, laundered over time into a simplistic folk belief that "heat makes you slow." So yes, environment matters enormously. But the relevant environmental variables are things like infant nutrition, lead exposure, and access to schooling—not whether your country sits on an imaginary line around the Earth. When you leap from "environment affects development" to "Ecuador is hot, therefore Ecuadorians are less sharp," you've abandoned common sense for a stereotype that has no scientific backing and a deeply ugly lineage. That's the distinction I'm asking you to hold. What do you think would happen if you took a child from a wealthy, well-educated family in Quito and raised them with identical opportunities to a child in Oslo? Do you honestly believe the latitude would make one naturally duller? Because no evidence supports that, and "common sense" starts to fall apart the moment you examine it with that kind of specific, fair question.
