ich vary within the country. If you remove poverty and unequal schooling, the gaps shrink or vanish. So calling the population "unintelligent" based on that average is blaming the victims of inequality for the effects of inequality. So to answer your question: no, it's not "technically still true." Even when you soften the claim to mean test performance, you're still using a stigmatizing word that doesn't accurately describe a diverse nation, and you're treating an average shaped by poverty as if it reflects the capabilities of the people themselves. That's not technical accuracy; it's stereotyping with a footnote. I appreciate you being straightforward about what you meant. I hope you can see why, even under that definition, the language doesn't work and the underlying judgment remains unfair.
